1
00:00:56,565 --> 00:00:59,359
I don't mean to hurry you.

2
00:00:59,484 --> 00:01:00,485
The prime minister did ask...

3
00:01:00,610 --> 00:01:01,862
to see you at once.

4
00:01:03,113 --> 00:01:04,573
He doesn't have long, sir.

5
00:01:05,615 --> 00:01:07,868
It's all right. I'm on my way.

6
00:01:07,951 --> 00:01:09,536
- Stephen.
- See you later.

7
00:01:16,794 --> 00:01:18,504
Dr. Fleming, Prime Minister.

8
00:01:18,629 --> 00:01:19,755
Ah, Stephen.

9
00:01:21,632 --> 00:01:24,427
- Welcome.
- Good afternoon, sir.

10
00:01:24,469 --> 00:01:25,845
I owe you a debt of gratitude...

11
00:01:25,970 --> 00:01:27,472
from what I'm told.

12
00:01:28,806 --> 00:01:30,308
Thank you.

13
00:01:30,433 --> 00:01:31,934
You saw off
this backbench rebellion...

14
00:01:32,018 --> 00:01:33,352
over the finance bill.

15
00:01:33,478 --> 00:01:35,563
Rebels went in breathing fire.

16
00:01:35,646 --> 00:01:37,065
Then they came out...

17
00:01:37,149 --> 00:01:38,692
purring like lambs.

18
00:01:38,817 --> 00:01:40,319
That's not quite true.

19
00:01:41,653 --> 00:01:42,696
How did you do it?

20
00:02:05,804 --> 00:02:06,930
Same time tomorrow, Raymond.

21
00:02:07,013 --> 00:02:07,931
Very well, sir.

22
00:02:08,014 --> 00:02:09,015
Good night.

23
00:02:12,185 --> 00:02:14,479
I'm sure she's very nice.
They all are.

24
00:02:14,604 --> 00:02:17,649
Very funny. You'll like her.

25
00:02:17,732 --> 00:02:18,733
Are you in for dinner?

26
00:02:19,984 --> 00:02:21,736
Your father's here.
I have to go.

27
00:02:23,446 --> 00:02:25,240
- Good evening, Beth.
- Good evening, sir.

28
00:02:25,365 --> 00:02:27,867
- Seen Sally?
- She's upstairs.

29
00:02:27,992 --> 00:02:29,870
I'm cooking, believe it or not.

30
00:02:29,995 --> 00:02:31,705
I've got the evening off.

31
00:02:31,789 --> 00:02:33,958
- Enjoy it.
- I will.

32
00:02:34,041 --> 00:02:36,335
Bye, Martyn. Speak to you soon.

33
00:02:36,502 --> 00:02:37,670
Any news?

34
00:02:39,713 --> 00:02:41,090
He's got a new girlfriend.

35
00:02:41,215 --> 00:02:43,968
Really?

36
00:02:44,051 --> 00:02:45,761
What does that mean?

37
00:02:45,886 --> 00:02:47,513
Why can't women see through him?

38
00:02:47,680 --> 00:02:49,765
He just wants to get his leg
over them.

39
00:02:51,142 --> 00:02:52,226
What?

40
00:02:56,481 --> 00:02:58,817
By the way, Edward phoned.

41
00:03:01,153 --> 00:03:02,863
I don't believe your father.

42
00:03:04,364 --> 00:03:07,534
He said the prime minister had
summoned you into his office.

43
00:03:11,705 --> 00:03:13,832
So?

44
00:03:13,915 --> 00:03:15,834
So... he just wanted to chat.

45
00:03:18,253 --> 00:03:21,715
He asked me whether power
meant anything to me.

46
00:03:21,840 --> 00:03:23,759
What did you say?

47
00:03:23,885 --> 00:03:25,636
The usual.

48
00:03:25,761 --> 00:03:27,805
I said my family
was far more important.

49
00:03:38,733 --> 00:03:40,401
Can you come and help me?

50
00:04:12,476 --> 00:04:15,480
God, this is rough.
Does anyone speak English?

51
00:04:15,605 --> 00:04:16,815
One or two.

52
00:04:17,649 --> 00:04:19,734
Why are the French
always so good at these things?

53
00:04:22,153 --> 00:04:24,280
We should be making tracks.

54
00:04:24,405 --> 00:04:26,282
One for the road.

55
00:04:26,366 --> 00:04:28,117
You know my situation.

56
00:04:28,159 --> 00:04:30,411
These days, I don't hurry home.

57
00:04:30,537 --> 00:04:31,746
One more...

58
00:04:32,997 --> 00:04:36,125
and then I really must go,
Donald.

59
00:04:36,167 --> 00:04:37,293
Whiskey?

60
00:05:05,823 --> 00:05:07,616
You're Martyn's father.

61
00:05:08,743 --> 00:05:09,870
I'm Anna Barton.

62
00:05:09,995 --> 00:05:13,123
I felt I ought
to introduce myself.

63
00:05:13,248 --> 00:05:14,332
How do you do?

64
00:05:16,877 --> 00:05:18,545
How long have you known him?

65
00:05:18,628 --> 00:05:20,505
Not very long.

66
00:05:21,590 --> 00:05:23,633
I see.

67
00:05:23,717 --> 00:05:24,843
I've...

68
00:05:26,344 --> 00:05:29,014
We've been close
just a few months.

69
00:05:34,395 --> 00:05:35,521
I've heard about you.

70
00:05:46,824 --> 00:05:48,034
I bumped into Tubby Haycroft.

71
00:05:48,159 --> 00:05:50,536
He's just been clobbered
in the spending review.

72
00:05:50,578 --> 00:05:51,621
Hang on a minute.

73
00:05:52,955 --> 00:05:55,041
Apparently,
the treasury's way out of order.

74
00:05:55,124 --> 00:05:56,876
He says
they're simply out of control.

75
00:05:59,128 --> 00:06:01,840
Poor old Tubby,
he's hopping mad.

76
00:06:01,924 --> 00:06:04,551
Of course, he says he's bored
with his department.

77
00:06:04,718 --> 00:06:08,222
I think the problem is
he can't stand his minister.

78
00:06:47,512 --> 00:06:49,055
Hello, stranger.

79
00:06:49,138 --> 00:06:52,183
I know. Don't make me feel bad.

80
00:06:52,308 --> 00:06:53,978
It's been hectic at the paper.

81
00:06:54,103 --> 00:06:56,272
Lovely to see you.

82
00:06:56,397 --> 00:06:58,065
You must be Anna.

83
00:06:58,149 --> 00:06:59,775
Anna Barton, my mother.

84
00:06:59,859 --> 00:07:00,901
How are you?

85
00:07:02,069 --> 00:07:03,863
Come on through.

86
00:07:09,660 --> 00:07:11,495
Hello, Sally. Hi.

87
00:07:13,080 --> 00:07:15,166
What are you doing here?

88
00:07:15,332 --> 00:07:17,334
This is
my horrible little sister.

89
00:07:17,459 --> 00:07:18,836
Lunch is all ready.

90
00:07:18,961 --> 00:07:21,339
We're only here for a drink.

91
00:07:21,464 --> 00:07:23,175
We can't stop for lunch.

92
00:07:23,300 --> 00:07:26,136
Say hello to your father.

93
00:07:26,261 --> 00:07:28,180
Hi, Dad. This is Anna.

94
00:07:28,263 --> 00:07:29,681
My father.

95
00:07:29,723 --> 00:07:32,475
Let's have a drink,
and I'll show you around.

96
00:07:33,935 --> 00:07:35,353
Nice to meet you.

97
00:07:36,521 --> 00:07:38,523
Have you been all right?

98
00:07:38,648 --> 00:07:40,525
Yes, mostly.

99
00:07:41,693 --> 00:07:43,528
Tied up a lot
with the children's home.

100
00:07:43,653 --> 00:07:45,530
It's turning into a proper job.

101
00:07:45,655 --> 00:07:49,285
People ask me to do things.
I find it difficult to say no.

102
00:07:53,039 --> 00:07:55,249
Is she all right?

103
00:07:55,374 --> 00:07:57,376
She hasn't said anything.

104
00:07:57,460 --> 00:08:00,588
Are we going to go around
the garden all day?

105
00:08:00,713 --> 00:08:02,006
We have to go.

106
00:08:06,886 --> 00:08:08,012
Thank you for the drink.

107
00:08:08,095 --> 00:08:10,181
Not at all.

108
00:08:10,306 --> 00:08:12,350
It's always nice
to meet Martyn's friends.

109
00:08:17,731 --> 00:08:18,857
It was lovely.

110
00:08:18,899 --> 00:08:20,901
It was nice to see you, Ma.

111
00:08:42,173 --> 00:08:43,466
Bye.

112
00:08:49,806 --> 00:08:51,099
It was funny.

113
00:08:51,266 --> 00:08:54,769
The great joke was
he didn't spot it at auction.

114
00:08:55,895 --> 00:08:58,022
It was staring him in the face.

115
00:08:58,106 --> 00:09:00,191
It was Delft.

116
00:09:00,316 --> 00:09:01,776
1640s.

117
00:09:01,860 --> 00:09:03,695
He would have thrown it away.

118
00:09:07,783 --> 00:09:09,285
Thank you, Tommy.

119
00:09:09,410 --> 00:09:11,704
- I'm off to London.
- See you later.

120
00:09:38,815 --> 00:09:40,192
Miss Snow here.

121
00:09:40,317 --> 00:09:42,611
My name's Anna Barton.

122
00:09:42,652 --> 00:09:44,070
I'm a friend of the minister's.

123
00:09:44,154 --> 00:09:47,032
I was wondering
if I could have a word.

124
00:09:47,115 --> 00:09:49,993
Hold on.
I'll see if the minister's here.

125
00:10:01,839 --> 00:10:03,716
A Miss Barton for you.

126
00:10:09,680 --> 00:10:10,806
Put her through.

127
00:10:18,981 --> 00:10:20,066
It's Anna.

128
00:10:25,239 --> 00:10:26,365
Give me your address.

129
00:10:28,909 --> 00:10:30,410
I'll be there in an hour.

130
00:11:03,778 --> 00:11:04,863
Raymond.

131
00:15:20,461 --> 00:15:23,006
Mind you,
hysteria is perfectly treatable.

132
00:15:25,550 --> 00:15:27,010
I'm not very pleased about it.

133
00:15:27,051 --> 00:15:30,471
We'll get the bill through.
No doubt about that.

134
00:15:30,597 --> 00:15:32,265
We've got some worried masters.

135
00:15:37,520 --> 00:15:38,605
Excuse me, Dr. Fleming.

136
00:16:01,587 --> 00:16:03,172
This is a rare pleasure.

137
00:16:03,297 --> 00:16:06,050
I was hoping
we might have a word.

138
00:16:16,978 --> 00:16:18,396
Over here.

139
00:16:21,483 --> 00:16:24,194
I've got a new job.

140
00:16:24,319 --> 00:16:27,239
I've been made
the deputy political editor.

141
00:16:30,116 --> 00:16:32,911
That's wonderful.

142
00:16:33,036 --> 00:16:34,079
Already?

143
00:16:34,204 --> 00:16:35,581
I know.

144
00:16:35,748 --> 00:16:37,583
That's extraordinary.
You got it so young.

145
00:16:37,708 --> 00:16:41,587
I know. I'm going to have
to write about you.

146
00:16:43,089 --> 00:16:44,215
Try to be kind.

147
00:16:44,340 --> 00:16:46,092
I'll try to be objective.

148
00:16:49,470 --> 00:16:52,348
Ma insisted I come down
and tell you.

149
00:16:52,431 --> 00:16:54,141
She said you'd be thrilled.

150
00:16:54,267 --> 00:16:55,810
She's right. I am.

151
00:16:57,436 --> 00:16:59,355
Who else knows about this?

152
00:16:59,438 --> 00:17:00,815
Only Anna.

153
00:17:03,443 --> 00:17:05,654
It's great news.

154
00:17:05,737 --> 00:17:07,823
Ma's saying
we all have to go celebrate.

155
00:17:07,948 --> 00:17:10,409
She's right. We must.

156
00:17:10,576 --> 00:17:13,453
Nothing too rich.

157
00:17:13,579 --> 00:17:15,706
That's very safe.

158
00:17:22,129 --> 00:17:23,881
Here they come.
Welcome the hero.

159
00:17:24,006 --> 00:17:25,507
Hello, Grandpa.

160
00:17:25,632 --> 00:17:27,009
Congratulations.

161
00:17:27,134 --> 00:17:29,470
Well done.

162
00:17:29,596 --> 00:17:30,930
You don't know Edward.

163
00:17:30,972 --> 00:17:32,891
My grandfather.

164
00:17:32,974 --> 00:17:34,976
Gracious.
Is this your new friend?

165
00:17:35,101 --> 00:17:36,811
I'm honored.
I've read all about you.

166
00:17:36,936 --> 00:17:40,106
Please. Mine was
a very overrated career.

167
00:17:42,817 --> 00:17:46,613
Let's face it,
we look to Stephen.

168
00:17:46,738 --> 00:17:49,616
I'm afraid it was me that
forced him into politics...

169
00:17:49,699 --> 00:17:52,327
me and Ingrid, at least.

170
00:17:52,452 --> 00:17:55,581
I don't think he'd have
chosen the life himself.

171
00:17:55,664 --> 00:17:56,749
What will you drink?

172
00:17:56,832 --> 00:17:58,417
Champagne.

173
00:17:58,501 --> 00:18:00,377
Champagne, indeed.

174
00:18:00,503 --> 00:18:03,130
Well done.

175
00:18:03,214 --> 00:18:05,466
Well done in every respect.

176
00:18:07,551 --> 00:18:08,969
I was brought up abroad.

177
00:18:09,053 --> 00:18:10,804
My father was a diplomat.

178
00:18:10,888 --> 00:18:12,640
We almost never lived here.

179
00:18:13,724 --> 00:18:15,476
My mother's sort of...

180
00:18:15,601 --> 00:18:17,144
She's very French.

181
00:18:18,187 --> 00:18:20,481
And now,
she's with her fourth husband...

182
00:18:20,606 --> 00:18:22,692
and lives in Palm Springs.

183
00:18:22,817 --> 00:18:26,279
Palm Springs? Good gracious.

184
00:18:27,364 --> 00:18:29,157
So it was quite
an odd childhood.

185
00:18:29,199 --> 00:18:30,450
We moved about, yes.

186
00:18:30,575 --> 00:18:33,495
The Argentine, Africa.

187
00:18:33,620 --> 00:18:34,829
For a long time,
we were in Rome.

188
00:18:34,955 --> 00:18:38,166
Then, I don't know,
I moved to Paris.

189
00:18:38,291 --> 00:18:40,794
I wanted to be free.

190
00:18:40,877 --> 00:18:43,672
I even had a spell
as a waitress.

191
00:18:44,631 --> 00:18:47,008
When you're young...

192
00:18:47,092 --> 00:18:48,427
Then I'd ended a relationship.

193
00:18:48,553 --> 00:18:51,055
I moved to England
about three years ago.

194
00:18:52,723 --> 00:18:53,724
You live by yourself?

195
00:18:57,061 --> 00:18:58,563
Do you see your mother?

196
00:18:58,688 --> 00:19:00,022
It's turning into a grilling.

197
00:19:00,106 --> 00:19:01,440
Not at all.

198
00:19:01,566 --> 00:19:03,067
Anna's here to have a good time.

199
00:19:03,150 --> 00:19:04,235
But I am.

200
00:19:09,907 --> 00:19:11,701
Were you an only child?

201
00:19:12,743 --> 00:19:13,828
I had a brother.

202
00:19:16,081 --> 00:19:18,750
Anna's brother died.

203
00:19:18,876 --> 00:19:20,878
He killed himself when I was 15.

204
00:19:21,003 --> 00:19:23,213
I'm sorry.

205
00:19:23,380 --> 00:19:26,175
- How old was he?
- Sixteen.

206
00:19:26,258 --> 00:19:28,135
Did you see his body?

207
00:19:29,511 --> 00:19:30,596
What was it like?

208
00:19:30,721 --> 00:19:32,347
Rough.

209
00:19:36,185 --> 00:19:37,269
Why did he kill himself?

210
00:19:40,189 --> 00:19:41,274
Love.

211
00:19:50,450 --> 00:19:52,327
I've got to go back
to the office.

212
00:19:52,452 --> 00:19:54,704
I've got work to finish.

213
00:19:54,787 --> 00:19:55,872
Could I have the bill?

214
00:19:59,459 --> 00:20:01,211
Thank you
for such a lovely evening.

215
00:20:01,294 --> 00:20:03,046
Thank you.

216
00:20:03,129 --> 00:20:04,172
Good night.

217
00:20:04,297 --> 00:20:05,757
Good night, everyone.

218
00:20:12,640 --> 00:20:15,184
I don't trust her.

219
00:20:15,309 --> 00:20:17,770
Don't worry. I don't think
it's anything serious.

220
00:20:17,811 --> 00:20:20,523
I'll take the car.

221
00:20:20,648 --> 00:20:23,108
You go home with Raymond.
I've got to go vote.

222
00:20:24,443 --> 00:20:25,528
See you.

223
00:20:28,656 --> 00:20:30,074
How's that tummy?

224
00:20:30,282 --> 00:20:32,076
Full.

225
00:20:32,159 --> 00:20:33,328
Will you be all right?

226
00:20:33,495 --> 00:20:36,581
I'll be all right.

227
00:20:36,665 --> 00:20:38,124
Sleep well.

228
00:20:46,675 --> 00:20:48,969
I'll see you tomorrow.

229
00:21:37,144 --> 00:21:39,771
We've got to find
a structure for this.

230
00:21:47,070 --> 00:21:49,406
All through dinner, I just...

231
00:21:49,573 --> 00:21:52,577
I wanted to touch you.

232
00:21:52,702 --> 00:21:55,163
I wanted to hold you.

233
00:21:57,540 --> 00:21:58,958
You must never worry.

234
00:21:59,083 --> 00:22:00,668
I'll always be there.

235
00:22:07,091 --> 00:22:08,551
Who are you?

236
00:25:37,560 --> 00:25:39,437
Tell me about Aston.

237
00:25:39,520 --> 00:25:41,105
My brother and I?

238
00:25:42,648 --> 00:25:44,817
We were always together.

239
00:25:45,943 --> 00:25:47,695
Every few years,
there was a new country...

240
00:25:47,820 --> 00:25:51,575
or there was
a new language to learn...

241
00:25:53,202 --> 00:25:56,580
so, of course, Aston and I
grew closer and closer.

242
00:25:58,707 --> 00:26:02,586
We had nothing else,
just each other.

243
00:26:04,838 --> 00:26:05,923
I loved him...

244
00:26:11,470 --> 00:26:15,349
but he couldn't face the fact
that I was going to grow up.

245
00:26:18,895 --> 00:26:21,022
He couldn't let go of me.

246
00:26:22,315 --> 00:26:24,192
He wanted me all for himself...

247
00:26:28,696 --> 00:26:32,283
so it's made me terrified
of any kind of possessiveness.

248
00:26:36,329 --> 00:26:37,789
You see...

249
00:26:38,873 --> 00:26:40,542
if you can imagine...

250
00:26:42,085 --> 00:26:45,339
the worst thing
that could happen ever...

251
00:26:45,464 --> 00:26:47,007
in the whole of your life...

252
00:26:48,384 --> 00:26:50,594
well, that happened to me.

253
00:26:53,973 --> 00:26:56,016
My brother
killed himself over me.

254
00:27:01,730 --> 00:27:03,315
I had to decide.

255
00:27:04,608 --> 00:27:08,863
I made up my mind because
I could have gone under...

256
00:27:11,783 --> 00:27:13,034
but I wasn't going to.

257
00:27:18,039 --> 00:27:19,207
Remember...

258
00:27:20,458 --> 00:27:22,127
damaged people are dangerous.

259
00:27:23,420 --> 00:27:24,754
They know they can survive.

260
00:27:36,141 --> 00:27:37,226
Listen.

261
00:27:40,104 --> 00:27:42,899
I'm going to Brussels
this weekend for a conference.

262
00:27:46,402 --> 00:27:47,570
Come with me.

263
00:27:50,865 --> 00:27:52,783
I'm spending the weekend
with Martyn.

264
00:27:57,622 --> 00:28:00,833
You know I'll do anything.

265
00:28:00,958 --> 00:28:02,376
I've shown you.

266
00:28:05,631 --> 00:28:07,549
Please let it be.

267
00:28:08,759 --> 00:28:09,885
Don't change it.

268
00:28:32,116 --> 00:28:33,243
Morning, my dear.

269
00:28:35,870 --> 00:28:37,121
You all right?

270
00:28:37,205 --> 00:28:38,289
Very much so.

271
00:28:43,127 --> 00:28:44,212
Are you all packed?

272
00:28:45,713 --> 00:28:46,839
Raymond's out there.

273
00:28:51,135 --> 00:28:53,304
You two be all right
on your own?

274
00:28:53,429 --> 00:28:54,806
I expect so.

275
00:28:57,851 --> 00:28:59,979
You can always call Martyn...

276
00:29:00,062 --> 00:29:01,730
get him to come over.

277
00:29:01,856 --> 00:29:03,732
Didn't I tell you?

278
00:29:03,858 --> 00:29:05,150
They're going to Paris.

279
00:29:06,694 --> 00:29:08,320
Paris?

280
00:29:08,445 --> 00:29:10,030
I told you it was serious.

281
00:29:10,155 --> 00:29:11,615
The Lutetia Hotel.

282
00:29:11,699 --> 00:29:12,950
In my experience...

283
00:29:13,033 --> 00:29:14,952
people only go to Paris
for one thing.

284
00:29:15,077 --> 00:29:17,538
Thank you.

285
00:29:17,621 --> 00:29:18,998
I'll tell you what that is.

286
00:29:19,081 --> 00:29:21,083
We don't need to know.

287
00:29:22,460 --> 00:29:23,795
Are you off?

288
00:29:25,881 --> 00:29:27,299
Without a kiss?

289
00:29:29,676 --> 00:29:31,261
I'll see you Monday.

290
00:29:31,386 --> 00:29:33,179
I'll get breakfast on the plane.

291
00:29:55,119 --> 00:29:57,788
Gentlemen,
it is 5:00 in the morning.

292
00:29:57,914 --> 00:30:01,375
It is clear we'll get
no further agreement.

293
00:30:01,542 --> 00:30:03,085
Therefore,
I would like to suggest...

294
00:30:03,169 --> 00:30:05,671
a resumption
after a short time...

295
00:30:05,796 --> 00:30:06,923
School's out.

296
00:30:07,048 --> 00:30:10,384
to rethink our position
after some much-needed sleep.

297
00:30:10,468 --> 00:30:12,595
The meeting is now adjourned.

298
00:30:17,851 --> 00:30:21,522
Thank God for that...
12 hours recess.

299
00:30:23,649 --> 00:30:25,067
I think I'll walk.

300
00:30:25,150 --> 00:30:26,568
Are you sure?

301
00:30:26,693 --> 00:30:28,195
Absolutely.
See Brussels at dawn.

302
00:30:28,320 --> 00:30:29,571
Come on.

303
00:30:29,696 --> 00:30:31,740
Really. I need the exercise.

304
00:30:31,823 --> 00:30:33,575
It's up to you.

305
00:30:33,742 --> 00:30:34,826
See you later.

306
00:30:57,141 --> 00:30:58,851
La gare, s'il vous plait,
pour Paris.

307
00:31:36,224 --> 00:31:38,184
Hotel Lutetia, bonjour.

308
00:31:38,351 --> 00:31:40,979
Mademoiselle Barton,
s'il vous plait.

309
00:31:41,062 --> 00:31:43,273
Mademoiselle Barton?

310
00:31:43,398 --> 00:31:45,650
Elle est avec
un Monsieur Fleming.

311
00:32:09,467 --> 00:32:10,885
C'est pour Mademoiselle Barton.

312
00:32:21,520 --> 00:32:24,065
Turn left outside the hotel.

313
00:32:25,358 --> 00:32:26,776
Take the first street
on the left.

314
00:32:26,901 --> 00:32:28,195
Go to the end.

315
00:32:30,072 --> 00:32:31,198
I'll be by the church.

316
00:32:32,658 --> 00:32:34,201
Bien. Merci.

317
00:33:45,149 --> 00:33:46,610
I love you.

318
00:33:49,321 --> 00:33:50,822
I know.

319
00:34:29,028 --> 00:34:31,656
I can't see past you.

320
00:34:34,158 --> 00:34:36,661
I think
you've never seen much at all.

321
00:34:50,008 --> 00:34:51,176
I have to go back.

322
00:34:56,181 --> 00:34:57,641
He's still asleep.

323
00:35:13,867 --> 00:35:15,869
Don't follow us... please.

324
00:35:37,224 --> 00:35:38,559
Hotel Lutetia, bonjour.

325
00:35:43,772 --> 00:35:46,066
I would like a room for the day.

326
00:35:46,191 --> 00:35:47,401
For one person?

327
00:35:52,072 --> 00:35:54,074
Et une petit dejeuner...

328
00:35:56,577 --> 00:35:58,120
Des oeufs de jambon...

329
00:35:58,204 --> 00:36:00,582
du cafe, du pain,
des croissants.

330
00:36:05,670 --> 00:36:06,796
Et aussi, s'il vous plait...

331
00:36:06,922 --> 00:36:08,757
une bonne bouteille
de vin rouge.

332
00:38:11,259 --> 00:38:13,553
Look who's here. How are you?

333
00:38:14,763 --> 00:38:15,805
Brussels all right?

334
00:38:15,931 --> 00:38:17,515
I was bringing Raymond in
for some breakfast.

335
00:38:17,641 --> 00:38:19,267
He had to meet me so early.

336
00:38:19,392 --> 00:38:20,644
I can do without.

337
00:38:22,103 --> 00:38:23,939
I'll do anything for you.

338
00:38:29,778 --> 00:38:30,904
Who's that?

339
00:38:31,947 --> 00:38:33,531
His name is Henry.

340
00:38:33,615 --> 00:38:35,951
He slept over this weekend.

341
00:38:36,076 --> 00:38:38,329
Sally's in love.

342
00:38:38,454 --> 00:38:40,122
In love?

343
00:38:43,626 --> 00:38:45,419
That's the last time
I leave this house.

344
00:38:50,633 --> 00:38:52,051
Check the mark.

345
00:38:52,134 --> 00:38:54,095
It is.

346
00:38:54,220 --> 00:38:55,304
Will you hand me that?

347
00:38:59,600 --> 00:39:01,727
- Hold on.
- This one?

348
00:39:01,811 --> 00:39:04,731
There's a call for you on three.

349
00:39:04,856 --> 00:39:05,941
"Seven-three-one"?

350
00:39:06,066 --> 00:39:07,150
That's the one.

351
00:39:17,661 --> 00:39:19,329
I need to see you.

352
00:39:19,454 --> 00:39:20,581
Of course.

353
00:39:21,957 --> 00:39:23,417
At lunchtime.

354
00:39:23,584 --> 00:39:24,668
Where?

355
00:39:25,836 --> 00:39:28,672
Somewhere public.

356
00:39:28,839 --> 00:39:30,383
Oh, my God. Again?

357
00:39:30,508 --> 00:39:32,886
It's not what you're thinking.

358
00:39:33,011 --> 00:39:35,388
It's a different matter.

359
00:39:35,513 --> 00:39:38,725
It's difficult to explain
over the phone.

360
00:39:38,850 --> 00:39:40,894
I'll tell you more
when I see you.

361
00:39:41,019 --> 00:39:42,896
I'll call you back later.

362
00:39:43,021 --> 00:39:44,314
All right.

363
00:39:49,152 --> 00:39:50,820
Would you sign these for me?

364
00:39:52,155 --> 00:39:53,907
Certainly.

365
00:40:05,711 --> 00:40:08,172
I brought some sandwiches.

366
00:40:08,214 --> 00:40:10,591
I thought it was going
to be a nice day.

367
00:40:16,222 --> 00:40:18,015
I've been thinking
about what we should do.

368
00:40:18,098 --> 00:40:19,183
Do?

369
00:40:20,893 --> 00:40:22,895
I have to leave Ingrid.

370
00:40:23,021 --> 00:40:24,064
There's no doubt about that.

371
00:40:24,189 --> 00:40:26,524
It's the right thing
for everybody.

372
00:40:28,818 --> 00:40:30,195
I can't go on...

373
00:40:31,821 --> 00:40:33,031
not like this.

374
00:40:33,073 --> 00:40:35,825
What happened in Paris...

375
00:40:38,411 --> 00:40:39,621
The way I behaved.

376
00:40:41,122 --> 00:40:42,999
I've never had feelings
like this.

377
00:40:45,669 --> 00:40:47,420
I have to get them
into some sort of...

378
00:40:49,089 --> 00:40:50,675
order.

379
00:40:53,803 --> 00:40:56,931
I know it'll be hard for Martyn.
He's fond of you.

380
00:40:57,056 --> 00:40:58,140
He loves me.

381
00:40:59,267 --> 00:41:02,812
I know, but he's young.
He'll get over it.

382
00:41:02,937 --> 00:41:04,564
He's your son.

383
00:41:04,730 --> 00:41:05,815
He'd hate you.

384
00:41:07,733 --> 00:41:08,943
He'd hate me for a while, but...

385
00:41:09,026 --> 00:41:11,988
You'd lose him.
You'd lose your own son?

386
00:41:13,114 --> 00:41:16,493
You'd also destroy the life
you've made with Ingrid.

387
00:41:16,618 --> 00:41:17,911
It's a good life.

388
00:41:19,162 --> 00:41:21,081
What you say doesn't make sense.

389
00:41:23,417 --> 00:41:24,668
How come you're so sure?

390
00:41:24,710 --> 00:41:27,588
Because in your heart,
you don't even want it.

391
00:41:27,671 --> 00:41:29,923
You want us to start
eating breakfast together?

392
00:41:30,007 --> 00:41:31,508
I'd like that.

393
00:41:31,633 --> 00:41:34,261
You'd like it
if we lived in the same house...

394
00:41:34,386 --> 00:41:35,929
read the papers together?

395
00:41:38,599 --> 00:41:41,268
What would you gain
if you left Ingrid?

396
00:41:41,351 --> 00:41:42,687
I'd gain you.

397
00:41:42,812 --> 00:41:45,773
You'd be gaining something
you already have.

398
00:42:03,124 --> 00:42:04,208
When can you see me?

399
00:42:07,378 --> 00:42:08,463
Thursday.

400
00:42:13,719 --> 00:42:16,639
Thursday at five o'clock.

401
00:42:21,268 --> 00:42:24,605
I feel very optimistic
about these new developments.

402
00:42:24,730 --> 00:42:28,776
We set some new targets
in the area of industrial waste.

403
00:42:28,901 --> 00:42:30,152
These are not just talk.

404
00:42:30,236 --> 00:42:33,280
These are targets which are
sensible and realistic.

405
00:42:33,364 --> 00:42:35,659
Are you saying
that some serious commitments...

406
00:42:35,700 --> 00:42:38,119
actually emerged
from the Brussels talks?

407
00:42:38,203 --> 00:42:39,621
Brussels was good.

408
00:42:39,704 --> 00:42:43,500
It laid out a European agenda.

409
00:42:43,625 --> 00:42:45,794
We have to face the fact
that toxic waste...

410
00:42:45,877 --> 00:42:49,089
is no respecter
of national frontiers.

411
00:42:49,214 --> 00:42:52,634
Problems such as acid rain,
ocean pollution...

412
00:42:52,759 --> 00:42:54,678
the diminishing ozone layer...

413
00:42:54,761 --> 00:42:58,181
cannot be tackled
by one country on its own.

414
00:42:58,223 --> 00:42:59,641
There must be cooperation...

415
00:42:59,766 --> 00:43:02,103
between the member states
of the community.

416
00:43:02,228 --> 00:43:04,438
Are you really trying
to make out...

417
00:43:04,522 --> 00:43:07,358
that for once Britain
isn't dragging its heels?

418
00:43:07,525 --> 00:43:09,694
I don't think we ever were.

419
00:43:09,735 --> 00:43:12,572
It's an image
we became saddled with...

420
00:43:12,697 --> 00:43:15,241
but when is an image
ever the truth?

421
00:43:23,416 --> 00:43:24,542
That was pretty good.

422
00:43:26,752 --> 00:43:28,130
Don't tell me you were watching.

423
00:43:28,213 --> 00:43:29,590
Of course.

424
00:43:29,756 --> 00:43:32,092
I never miss a performance.

425
00:43:34,344 --> 00:43:35,679
You are extraordinary.

426
00:43:37,723 --> 00:43:39,141
So are you.

427
00:43:43,395 --> 00:43:44,646
It's a real gift.

428
00:43:46,231 --> 00:43:47,566
Being good at things.

429
00:43:50,819 --> 00:43:52,696
Somehow I remember
what I first saw in you...

430
00:43:54,949 --> 00:43:56,367
when you were a doctor...

431
00:43:58,244 --> 00:44:00,246
doing simple things well.

432
00:44:05,877 --> 00:44:07,211
I do love that.

433
00:44:13,134 --> 00:44:14,677
I still love that.

434
00:44:52,551 --> 00:44:54,594
I was expecting you.
Please come on in.

435
00:44:58,682 --> 00:45:00,934
Do you know Peter Wetzlar?

436
00:45:01,017 --> 00:45:03,478
I don't.

437
00:45:03,603 --> 00:45:05,313
This is Martyn's father.

438
00:45:07,482 --> 00:45:08,567
Peter stopped by.

439
00:45:12,195 --> 00:45:13,655
He lives in Paris.

440
00:45:22,915 --> 00:45:24,875
What brings you to London?

441
00:45:26,711 --> 00:45:28,129
The museum.

442
00:45:31,382 --> 00:45:33,134
What particular field?

443
00:45:33,217 --> 00:45:35,511
Chinese artifacts.

444
00:45:35,678 --> 00:45:37,096
The Han dynasty.

445
00:45:38,222 --> 00:45:40,057
He hasn't changed.

446
00:45:40,182 --> 00:45:42,561
He never warns you when
he's going to drop by.

447
00:45:42,728 --> 00:45:44,354
I didn't know
until the last moment...

448
00:45:44,438 --> 00:45:47,357
so I'm riding my luck,
as usual.

449
00:45:50,736 --> 00:45:52,195
I'll go and get your book.

450
00:45:52,321 --> 00:45:53,447
I'm sorry?

451
00:45:54,531 --> 00:45:56,366
Stephen's come around
for this book.

452
00:45:56,491 --> 00:45:58,201
It's out of print.

453
00:45:58,327 --> 00:46:00,203
I won't be a moment.
I put it aside.

454
00:46:06,920 --> 00:46:08,004
You know Anna well?

455
00:46:09,923 --> 00:46:11,591
I mean...

456
00:46:11,716 --> 00:46:13,551
we've been through
quite a lot together.

457
00:46:15,887 --> 00:46:17,222
She's still my best friend,
I guess.

458
00:46:18,890 --> 00:46:21,893
I liked your son.

459
00:46:22,018 --> 00:46:23,102
You met him?

460
00:46:24,520 --> 00:46:26,189
In Paris.

461
00:46:26,314 --> 00:46:30,777
It was important, I think,
for Anna.

462
00:46:31,903 --> 00:46:33,071
How do you mean?

463
00:46:33,154 --> 00:46:34,406
You know how it is.

464
00:46:34,573 --> 00:46:37,076
You feel you have to bring
all the bits together.

465
00:46:38,410 --> 00:46:41,080
I don't think I quite
know how you mean.

466
00:46:41,163 --> 00:46:43,832
She's getting
pretty serious with Martyn.

467
00:46:43,958 --> 00:46:45,125
She loves him.

468
00:46:46,418 --> 00:46:47,711
I'm sure
it's a very good thing...

469
00:46:47,795 --> 00:46:51,465
because he seems to know
how to handle her...

470
00:46:51,590 --> 00:46:54,426
to give her the freedom
she really needs.

471
00:46:55,886 --> 00:46:57,012
I never could.

472
00:46:59,473 --> 00:47:00,475
I see.

473
00:47:01,476 --> 00:47:04,104
So perhaps she'll be happy.

474
00:47:04,145 --> 00:47:06,147
God knows she deserves it.

475
00:47:06,231 --> 00:47:07,315
She does.

476
00:47:08,775 --> 00:47:11,069
- What's the book?
- Book?

477
00:47:11,152 --> 00:47:13,029
You came to pick up a book.

478
00:47:13,154 --> 00:47:14,489
It's just something I...

479
00:47:14,656 --> 00:47:16,283
Here it is.

480
00:47:16,366 --> 00:47:18,034
Thank you.

481
00:47:18,118 --> 00:47:19,536
This is it.

482
00:47:20,745 --> 00:47:21,997
I couldn't get it.

483
00:47:22,122 --> 00:47:23,290
Have it for as long as you like.

484
00:47:23,415 --> 00:47:24,541
Thank you.

485
00:47:25,834 --> 00:47:28,129
I won't intrude any longer.

486
00:47:28,212 --> 00:47:29,755
I'm sorry.
I didn't mean to bust in.

487
00:47:29,839 --> 00:47:31,132
Not at all.

488
00:47:33,342 --> 00:47:34,635
Very nice to meet you.

489
00:47:34,677 --> 00:47:35,761
Nice meeting you.

490
00:47:37,555 --> 00:47:41,309
I hope we all see you sometime.

491
00:48:38,701 --> 00:48:39,994
Thank you for the book.

492
00:48:41,871 --> 00:48:43,122
What was that all about?

493
00:48:49,546 --> 00:48:50,631
Who is that man?

494
00:48:56,428 --> 00:48:59,056
I was with Peter
the night Aston died.

495
00:49:01,809 --> 00:49:04,186
Peter's someone
I'll know forever.

496
00:49:13,238 --> 00:49:15,073
Our parents were friends.

497
00:49:15,156 --> 00:49:16,408
We'd had the same life.

498
00:49:18,785 --> 00:49:20,870
One evening,
he took me out dancing.

499
00:49:22,664 --> 00:49:24,708
I was 15.

500
00:49:25,834 --> 00:49:28,587
Peter brought me home
in his car...

501
00:49:28,712 --> 00:49:30,714
and he started to kiss me.

502
00:49:35,969 --> 00:49:38,848
I didn't know Aston
was watching.

503
00:49:38,973 --> 00:49:41,934
Aston was watching
from a window upstairs.

504
00:49:43,394 --> 00:49:47,064
When I went in,
he started to go mad.

505
00:49:47,106 --> 00:49:50,109
"You'll let him fuck you.
They're all going to," he said.

506
00:49:50,234 --> 00:49:52,111
"It's the end for us."

507
00:49:54,655 --> 00:49:56,574
He came to my room.

508
00:49:56,699 --> 00:49:59,243
He wanted to sleep in my bed.

509
00:49:59,368 --> 00:50:01,996
All I knew was
I had to get him out.

510
00:50:02,079 --> 00:50:03,456
I locked the door.

511
00:50:05,042 --> 00:50:07,085
I heard him outside.

512
00:50:07,169 --> 00:50:09,588
He started imploring me,
begging me...

513
00:50:09,713 --> 00:50:11,924
saying he was sorry,
would I forgive him?

514
00:50:12,007 --> 00:50:13,675
I just listened.

515
00:50:17,054 --> 00:50:19,056
For hours, I listened.

516
00:50:21,683 --> 00:50:23,143
Then it stopped.

517
00:50:25,896 --> 00:50:26,980
I went to sleep.

518
00:50:29,149 --> 00:50:33,529
Next morning, I woke, and...
I heard crying.

519
00:50:33,696 --> 00:50:35,114
I went down the corridor.

520
00:50:35,198 --> 00:50:37,992
They all told me not to go in,
but I did...

521
00:50:39,994 --> 00:50:41,996
and I saw Aston.

522
00:50:48,044 --> 00:50:49,671
He'd cut his wrists.

523
00:50:59,723 --> 00:51:01,850
I took his head in my arms.

524
00:51:04,186 --> 00:51:06,522
His face had been
under the water...

525
00:51:07,815 --> 00:51:09,149
and the water was red.

526
00:51:14,071 --> 00:51:15,197
They said we had to leave.

527
00:51:15,239 --> 00:51:17,491
They took me and my mother
to stay with Peter's parents.

528
00:51:17,616 --> 00:51:19,868
They wanted us out of the house.

529
00:51:21,412 --> 00:51:22,871
Peter was waiting.

530
00:51:24,249 --> 00:51:25,584
He took me upstairs.

531
00:51:27,544 --> 00:51:30,338
My nightdress was still
covered with blood.

532
00:51:33,717 --> 00:51:35,594
It was then...

533
00:51:38,221 --> 00:51:40,682
I had to. I knew I had to.

534
00:51:43,143 --> 00:51:44,978
I asked him to fuck me.

535
00:51:48,273 --> 00:51:49,691
"Fuck me," I said.

536
00:52:28,482 --> 00:52:29,608
It's all right.

537
00:53:58,033 --> 00:53:59,993
I don't mind her being there.

538
00:54:00,076 --> 00:54:02,329
I really don't mind.

539
00:54:02,454 --> 00:54:06,167
It's just...
I wish Martyn had asked us.

540
00:54:08,294 --> 00:54:11,714
Suddenly she seems to be part
of our lives, which is fine.

541
00:54:12,924 --> 00:54:14,175
It's just this weekend.

542
00:54:14,258 --> 00:54:18,930
It's Hartley, which Martyn knows
is my favorite place on Earth...

543
00:54:19,055 --> 00:54:20,556
and it's my birthday.

544
00:54:22,433 --> 00:54:25,353
It makes me sound mean,
which I hate.

545
00:54:27,730 --> 00:54:30,859
I don't know.
She brings out the worst in me.

546
00:54:32,486 --> 00:54:34,238
Don't worry. It'll be fine.

547
00:54:38,033 --> 00:54:41,495
This is a portrait of Ingrid's
great-great-grandmother.

548
00:54:42,871 --> 00:54:44,706
Was Ingrid brought up here?

549
00:54:44,790 --> 00:54:46,375
Of course.

550
00:54:46,500 --> 00:54:49,544
There have been Lloyds here
for over 200 years.

551
00:54:49,586 --> 00:54:53,006
Ingrid's my only child,
so it falls now to Martyn.

552
00:54:53,131 --> 00:54:56,511
Martyn and his family
will inherit this place.

553
00:54:56,636 --> 00:54:57,762
Meanwhile, I'm by myself.

554
00:54:57,845 --> 00:55:00,014
That's why these weekends
are so wonderful.

555
00:55:00,139 --> 00:55:01,849
Grandpa!

556
00:55:01,975 --> 00:55:06,104
I assume you'll be back many
times and always be welcome.

557
00:55:06,187 --> 00:55:07,605
Won't she, Ingrid?

558
00:55:08,439 --> 00:55:09,857
Thank you, darling.

559
00:55:12,151 --> 00:55:14,862
This is the most
extraordinary place.

560
00:55:16,948 --> 00:55:19,409
You're not convinced?

561
00:55:19,492 --> 00:55:22,621
I was always made to come here
for the holidays.

562
00:55:22,746 --> 00:55:25,499
That isn't fair.

563
00:55:27,626 --> 00:55:29,670
While, of course,
the company was fine...

564
00:55:29,795 --> 00:55:30,963
I should hope.

565
00:55:31,046 --> 00:55:33,507
my childhood
wasn't all wonderful.

566
00:55:33,632 --> 00:55:35,551
Really?

567
00:55:39,138 --> 00:55:41,223
As a matter of fact, it wasn't.

568
00:55:42,308 --> 00:55:45,811
Perhaps... I don't know,
perhaps it was...

569
00:55:45,978 --> 00:55:47,646
Perhaps it was too perfect...

570
00:55:47,730 --> 00:55:49,983
nothing was to disturb
the surface of things.

571
00:55:50,108 --> 00:55:52,193
Everything was fine...

572
00:55:52,277 --> 00:55:54,029
no questions.

573
00:55:55,530 --> 00:55:56,615
It isn't healthy.

574
00:56:00,744 --> 00:56:02,704
That's all I'm saying.
It's not a big issue.

575
00:56:02,829 --> 00:56:04,289
But it's good to have roots.

576
00:56:04,414 --> 00:56:06,666
But roots aren't that great...

577
00:56:06,750 --> 00:56:08,668
unless something else
comes with them.

578
00:56:10,879 --> 00:56:12,964
- Like what?
- I don't know.

579
00:56:13,089 --> 00:56:15,551
Warmth, I suppose.

580
00:56:16,677 --> 00:56:17,803
Passion.

581
00:56:20,097 --> 00:56:21,933
It was probably my fault.

582
00:56:24,143 --> 00:56:26,729
It came more from Dad,
if I have to name anyone.

583
00:56:40,409 --> 00:56:43,372
They make a wonderful couple,
don't they?

584
00:56:43,455 --> 00:56:44,706
Granddad.

585
00:56:47,459 --> 00:56:49,670
Good night, you old devil.

586
00:56:52,798 --> 00:56:54,716
You'll sleep well in this room.

587
00:56:54,800 --> 00:56:56,885
It's one of my favorites.

588
00:57:44,768 --> 00:57:46,604
Right, now concentrate.

589
00:57:46,687 --> 00:57:50,107
Put your arm here,
hand on there, and look there...

590
00:57:50,149 --> 00:57:51,609
and look up.

591
00:57:53,861 --> 00:57:55,279
Close.

592
00:57:55,362 --> 00:57:56,655
Come on here.

593
00:58:00,660 --> 00:58:01,953
Had a good walk?

594
00:58:05,624 --> 00:58:06,708
Who's winning?

595
00:58:06,791 --> 00:58:09,085
I think Martyn
is winning everything.

596
00:58:19,220 --> 00:58:20,680
This house was full...

597
00:58:20,805 --> 00:58:23,725
It was full every weekend.

598
00:58:23,808 --> 00:58:24,976
There we are.

599
00:58:25,060 --> 00:58:26,353
I thought Bob Marley was dead.

600
00:58:26,519 --> 00:58:27,730
Of course he's dead.

601
00:58:27,813 --> 00:58:29,482
Then why...

602
00:58:29,565 --> 00:58:31,400
Ingrid's mother
loved entertaining.

603
00:58:31,525 --> 00:58:32,568
Happy birthday.

604
00:58:32,693 --> 00:58:37,031
Politicians, of course,
and artists came, writers also.

605
00:58:37,156 --> 00:58:40,243
Boundless hospitality!

606
00:58:40,368 --> 00:58:43,162
My wife understood that.

607
00:58:43,204 --> 00:58:46,374
Life's pretty empty without her.

608
00:58:46,540 --> 00:58:48,584
That's why I'm so pleased...

609
00:58:48,709 --> 00:58:50,795
I'm so pleased for both of you.

610
00:58:52,380 --> 00:58:53,464
Am I going too far?

611
00:58:53,548 --> 00:58:55,259
Not yet, but you may soon.

612
00:58:55,384 --> 00:58:57,261
I doubt it.

613
00:58:57,386 --> 00:59:00,514
Come along,
I think we've all guessed.

614
00:59:00,639 --> 00:59:04,351
I assumed that's why you brought
Anna along.

615
00:59:04,476 --> 00:59:05,602
Please!

616
00:59:05,727 --> 00:59:08,021
Can't you see you're
embarrassing Martyn and Anna?

617
00:59:08,105 --> 00:59:11,817
Not at all. Far from it.

618
00:59:13,026 --> 00:59:15,904
Since Grandfather has said...

619
00:59:16,029 --> 00:59:18,490
I've just asked Anna
to marry me...

620
00:59:18,657 --> 00:59:22,704
and I'm pleased to say
she said yes.

621
00:59:27,417 --> 00:59:28,960
Oh, my God.

622
00:59:34,757 --> 00:59:38,595
It's really marvelous.

623
00:59:41,806 --> 00:59:43,975
We both think that's wonderful.

624
00:59:44,058 --> 00:59:45,226
Congratulations, my dear.

625
00:59:45,351 --> 00:59:47,896
Thank you. It's very sudden.

626
00:59:48,939 --> 00:59:50,232
We're taken aback.

627
00:59:50,357 --> 00:59:51,609
But you're pleased?

628
00:59:53,819 --> 00:59:55,195
Of course.

629
00:59:56,363 --> 00:59:57,656
Delighted.

630
00:59:58,866 --> 01:00:00,910
I do mean for both of you.

631
01:00:01,035 --> 01:00:02,369
Congratulations...
I'm sorry.

632
01:00:02,494 --> 01:00:03,913
Clumsy. All right.

633
01:00:03,996 --> 01:00:06,248
I was always sure
you two had this in mind.

634
01:00:06,373 --> 01:00:09,668
I'm pleased I've hastened
the announcement.

635
01:00:09,793 --> 01:00:11,420
You have.

636
01:00:12,546 --> 01:00:13,924
Let's drink a toast.

637
01:00:14,049 --> 01:00:17,552
Come on, children...
to Martyn and Anna.

638
01:00:29,064 --> 01:00:30,440
When did you ask?

639
01:00:32,150 --> 01:00:33,234
When did you ask Anna?

640
01:00:33,401 --> 01:00:36,279
It was a few days ago.

641
01:00:37,781 --> 01:00:39,617
She accepted straight-away?

642
01:00:45,831 --> 01:00:48,709
I know I've hurt you.

643
01:00:49,877 --> 01:00:51,295
All that stuff
at dinner last night.

644
01:00:51,420 --> 01:00:53,422
I don't know what that was.

645
01:00:53,506 --> 01:00:54,632
It's all right.

646
01:00:54,715 --> 01:00:57,385
The funny thing is that today
I feel very close to you.

647
01:00:58,970 --> 01:01:00,054
Heads or tails?

648
01:01:01,097 --> 01:01:02,223
Tails.

649
01:01:03,641 --> 01:01:04,850
Tails.

650
01:01:06,728 --> 01:01:09,147
And Mum...
I know I'm hurting her, but...

651
01:01:09,189 --> 01:01:11,984
whoever I married,
it was going to be hard.

652
01:01:12,067 --> 01:01:14,611
I can tell
she's worried about Anna.

653
01:01:16,363 --> 01:01:20,158
I think she's worried
you're taking on a lot.

654
01:01:20,242 --> 01:01:23,745
But you have to see that
that's why I'm drawn to her.

655
01:01:23,870 --> 01:01:28,166
There's a sadness... in her.

656
01:01:28,250 --> 01:01:31,545
I don't know why,
but it's very interesting.

657
01:01:34,549 --> 01:01:36,843
I've discovered I can help her,
and that's great.

658
01:01:36,926 --> 01:01:38,344
It's the best feeling.

659
01:01:39,762 --> 01:01:41,097
Makes you sound like a nurse.

660
01:01:46,394 --> 01:01:49,397
I'm not trying to stop you.

661
01:01:49,522 --> 01:01:51,065
I like Anna.
I really do...

662
01:01:51,190 --> 01:01:57,030
but are you sure
she's really what you want?

663
01:01:57,155 --> 01:01:59,617
You don't know her.

664
01:01:59,700 --> 01:02:01,410
There's a whole side to her...

665
01:02:01,577 --> 01:02:03,329
that's only there
when we're alone.

666
01:02:03,412 --> 01:02:06,165
If you could see that,
then you'd feel differently.

667
01:02:10,711 --> 01:02:13,756
When we're alone,
she's like no one else.

668
01:05:37,759 --> 01:05:39,636
Sir, I tried to find
all the people you asked for.

669
01:05:39,761 --> 01:05:40,804
I hope so.

670
01:05:40,929 --> 01:05:42,598
- Sir, will you need me?
- I need everybody.

671
01:05:43,849 --> 01:05:45,058
Good morning, sir.

672
01:05:50,981 --> 01:05:53,275
Do you know
what I think of that?

673
01:05:53,400 --> 01:05:56,236
Sir, before we start,
I'd like to apologize...

674
01:05:56,278 --> 01:05:59,407
This is supposed to be
a final draft...

675
01:05:59,532 --> 01:06:02,076
a ministry position paper...

676
01:06:02,160 --> 01:06:04,787
and it's full
of major factual errors.

677
01:06:07,165 --> 01:06:10,126
But what really irritates me
is that I'm the one...

678
01:06:10,251 --> 01:06:12,003
who has to stand up
in Parliament...

679
01:06:12,128 --> 01:06:14,547
and if I've been
wrongly briefed...

680
01:06:14,672 --> 01:06:18,301
then it's me
that gets it in the neck.

681
01:06:19,594 --> 01:06:22,013
It's all very well for you lot.
You shelter behind the woodwork.

682
01:06:22,138 --> 01:06:23,515
You wander in here and say...

683
01:06:23,641 --> 01:06:26,226
"Sorry, we slipped up
on the ozone figures..."

684
01:06:26,310 --> 01:06:27,728
but it's my job!

685
01:06:28,979 --> 01:06:30,856
Has that occurred to you?

686
01:06:30,981 --> 01:06:33,525
If I hadn't spotted
your ball's up...

687
01:06:33,651 --> 01:06:34,860
then my job
would've been on the line.

688
01:06:34,944 --> 01:06:36,278
Do you know that?

689
01:06:39,114 --> 01:06:41,533
You're redrafting?

690
01:06:41,659 --> 01:06:43,494
- Already.
- Good.

691
01:06:48,165 --> 01:06:50,502
That's it. I'm done.

692
01:07:00,720 --> 01:07:02,097
- Good evening, sir.
- Good evening.

693
01:07:18,364 --> 01:07:19,490
Where's your mother?

694
01:07:21,075 --> 01:07:22,994
Work, I suppose.
I haven't seen her.

695
01:07:24,787 --> 01:07:25,871
No Henry tonight?

696
01:07:32,086 --> 01:07:34,672
Sal... I wanted a word.

697
01:07:37,008 --> 01:07:38,843
You know when I saw you
at Hartley the other night...

698
01:07:38,926 --> 01:07:40,511
on the stairs?

699
01:07:42,389 --> 01:07:46,518
I'd had to talk to Anna.

700
01:07:46,602 --> 01:07:48,020
That's what I was doing.

701
01:07:50,063 --> 01:07:51,356
Your mum's a bit upset.

702
01:07:51,481 --> 01:07:54,192
You've probably sensed that.

703
01:07:55,444 --> 01:07:57,362
And...

704
01:07:57,487 --> 01:08:00,198
I was trying to bring
all the parties together.

705
01:08:10,418 --> 01:08:11,544
I'm going to bed.

706
01:08:26,559 --> 01:08:28,686
Oh, dear. Not a bit.

707
01:08:28,770 --> 01:08:30,688
I just love it.

708
01:08:30,772 --> 01:08:33,483
Would you at least choose
the music for the wedding?

709
01:08:33,608 --> 01:08:34,692
You should.

710
01:08:34,776 --> 01:08:36,695
I suppose so.

711
01:08:36,779 --> 01:08:38,447
She doesn't trust me, though.

712
01:08:38,530 --> 01:08:39,990
I'm so sorry I'm late.

713
01:08:40,115 --> 01:08:41,325
That's all right.

714
01:08:41,450 --> 01:08:44,286
- We started without you.
- Good.

715
01:08:44,411 --> 01:08:45,871
Mrs. Prideaux,
welcome to London.

716
01:08:45,955 --> 01:08:48,374
Thank you. How do you do?

717
01:08:49,458 --> 01:08:50,584
How are you?

718
01:08:52,211 --> 01:08:55,839
I was just saying I am
so thrilled at this occasion.

719
01:08:55,965 --> 01:08:58,384
Thrilled for Anna, of course.

720
01:08:58,467 --> 01:08:59,593
One way or another...

721
01:08:59,718 --> 01:09:01,553
I haven't seen
much of her lately...

722
01:09:01,638 --> 01:09:04,724
so it always moves me
so much when I do.

723
01:09:06,268 --> 01:09:09,145
Thank you.

724
01:09:09,271 --> 01:09:11,231
And one look at Martyn...

725
01:09:11,314 --> 01:09:13,441
- My goodness.
- I know.

726
01:09:14,985 --> 01:09:16,444
I feel flattered.

727
01:09:17,779 --> 01:09:20,490
Though, of course,
he's not Anna's usual type.

728
01:09:21,491 --> 01:09:22,575
I'm sorry?

729
01:09:24,327 --> 01:09:26,079
If I may say that.

730
01:09:26,162 --> 01:09:29,208
Anna's first boyfriend
did set a sort of pattern...

731
01:09:29,333 --> 01:09:32,003
tormented, difficult.

732
01:09:33,170 --> 01:09:36,007
And all the others
have followed from there.

733
01:09:36,173 --> 01:09:37,633
Are you talking about Peter?

734
01:09:41,012 --> 01:09:43,347
I've met him.
He seems very nice.

735
01:09:43,472 --> 01:09:44,807
He is.

736
01:09:46,309 --> 01:09:47,602
I miss him.

737
01:09:51,480 --> 01:09:53,232
Mother, everything's fine.

738
01:09:59,197 --> 01:10:00,240
How's the salmon?

739
01:10:00,365 --> 01:10:01,658
Good.

740
01:10:06,079 --> 01:10:08,332
When Peter and Anna broke up...

741
01:10:08,457 --> 01:10:11,710
Peter got married
to a brilliant young lawyer...

742
01:10:11,793 --> 01:10:13,587
a striking-looking girl...

743
01:10:13,712 --> 01:10:15,672
but it only lasted eight weeks.

744
01:10:15,797 --> 01:10:18,216
I can't boast.

745
01:10:18,258 --> 01:10:20,719
My own record
isn't much better than that.

746
01:10:20,802 --> 01:10:22,722
You've been married a few times?

747
01:10:25,475 --> 01:10:27,018
- 4.
- Good Lord!

748
01:10:27,101 --> 01:10:29,562
And one nearly in Reno.

749
01:10:29,687 --> 01:10:32,357
We went as far
as the Chapel of Love...

750
01:10:32,440 --> 01:10:34,067
and then I got cold feet.

751
01:10:34,150 --> 01:10:37,320
Thank goodness. Otherwise,
it would have been five.

752
01:10:41,741 --> 01:10:44,243
Of course, it's hard for us.

753
01:10:44,285 --> 01:10:45,954
I'm sure Anna has told you.

754
01:10:48,749 --> 01:10:49,833
Told us?

755
01:10:51,293 --> 01:10:52,920
About the brother she had.

756
01:11:06,517 --> 01:11:08,769
Of course, it strikes me.

757
01:11:08,894 --> 01:11:12,523
It had to strike me,
looking at Martyn.

758
01:11:14,442 --> 01:11:17,112
He does look
so like Anna's brother.

759
01:11:25,203 --> 01:11:27,247
I don't know
if you realized that.

760
01:11:33,586 --> 01:11:36,298
Not, of course,
that Aston was as handsome.

761
01:11:36,381 --> 01:11:39,301
Why do you always
make it worse for me?

762
01:11:39,426 --> 01:11:41,053
Do you think
it isn't bad enough?

763
01:11:41,137 --> 01:11:44,015
Do you think I constantly
need to be reminded?

764
01:11:47,476 --> 01:11:49,312
I'm sorry, I'm sure.

765
01:11:52,648 --> 01:11:54,984
Perhaps this is why
we shouldn't meet.

766
01:12:04,952 --> 01:12:06,830
It's all right.

767
01:12:08,415 --> 01:12:09,666
Really, it's fine.

768
01:12:32,522 --> 01:12:34,817
I think I made a bad impression.

769
01:12:34,901 --> 01:12:38,404
I'm afraid your wife
didn't look very pleased.

770
01:12:38,529 --> 01:12:40,281
I was nervous.
That was the trouble.

771
01:12:40,365 --> 01:12:42,951
It was fine, really.
We both enjoyed meeting you.

772
01:12:43,034 --> 01:12:44,911
But I'm not nervous now.

773
01:12:46,621 --> 01:12:50,375
You must realize,
this is a chance for Anna.

774
01:12:50,500 --> 01:12:52,251
At last, with Martyn,
she has a chance.

775
01:12:52,335 --> 01:12:53,878
She can make a new life.

776
01:12:55,129 --> 01:12:58,299
It would be too bad
if anything prevented her.

777
01:13:00,844 --> 01:13:02,721
I'm not sure I understand
what you mean.

778
01:13:04,348 --> 01:13:05,808
I think you do.

779
01:13:06,850 --> 01:13:09,061
I watched you
throughout the meal.

780
01:13:10,145 --> 01:13:11,897
You can't even look at her.

781
01:13:16,735 --> 01:13:18,862
You must get out of the way.

782
01:13:20,823 --> 01:13:22,408
I'm asking you.

783
01:13:24,493 --> 01:13:25,744
Please.

784
01:13:47,100 --> 01:13:48,602
I won't speak of this again.

785
01:13:50,520 --> 01:13:51,605
Thank you.

786
01:13:56,986 --> 01:13:58,904
The ministry, sir?

787
01:14:00,281 --> 01:14:01,907
Just drive around for a while.

788
01:14:20,469 --> 01:14:22,387
I need to get out.

789
01:14:34,399 --> 01:14:35,609
I won't be a minute.

790
01:14:50,833 --> 01:14:52,210
It's me. Don't say anything.

791
01:14:52,335 --> 01:14:53,670
I don't want you
to say anything.

792
01:14:56,339 --> 01:14:57,799
I have to let you go.

793
01:15:00,176 --> 01:15:03,304
It's the right thing to do
for Martyn and for you.

794
01:15:05,848 --> 01:15:07,517
We both know
it's the right thing.

795
01:15:08,643 --> 01:15:09,769
It's got to stop.

796
01:15:12,273 --> 01:15:13,857
I am sorry.

797
01:15:36,588 --> 01:15:38,758
- To the ministry now, sir?
- City Road.

798
01:15:54,190 --> 01:15:55,734
Do you know where I can find
Martyn Fleming?

799
01:16:00,530 --> 01:16:01,531
Over here.

800
01:16:04,869 --> 01:16:06,370
- Thank you.
- I'll tell him you're here.

801
01:16:27,767 --> 01:16:28,893
Why don't you have it?

802
01:16:30,436 --> 01:16:31,521
May I?

803
01:16:32,689 --> 01:16:33,941
Are you sure?

804
01:16:35,275 --> 01:16:38,070
What brings you here?

805
01:16:39,821 --> 01:16:42,741
Sentiment.

806
01:16:50,332 --> 01:16:51,416
Feeling...

807
01:16:56,546 --> 01:16:58,299
that I haven't behaved
as I should.

808
01:17:05,765 --> 01:17:10,186
You know what you were saying
in Hartley about... passion?

809
01:17:12,188 --> 01:17:13,856
You were right.

810
01:17:15,525 --> 01:17:17,527
I was distant.

811
01:17:17,652 --> 01:17:19,529
I know I was.

812
01:17:20,655 --> 01:17:24,243
I thought
you could control life...

813
01:17:27,162 --> 01:17:29,290
but it's not like that.

814
01:17:30,499 --> 01:17:31,625
There are things you...

815
01:17:34,503 --> 01:17:36,505
There are things
you can't control.

816
01:17:37,923 --> 01:17:39,800
That's right.

817
01:17:43,053 --> 01:17:44,471
Somehow you know that.

818
01:17:48,017 --> 01:17:50,019
I came to wish you both
good luck.

819
01:17:51,563 --> 01:17:52,772
Are these for me?

820
01:17:58,028 --> 01:18:00,155
I really appreciate this.

821
01:18:00,280 --> 01:18:01,823
It's very kind. Thank you.

822
01:18:08,038 --> 01:18:10,582
The piece doesn't fit,
that's the problem.

823
01:18:10,707 --> 01:18:12,667
We need something out...
200 words or so.

824
01:18:12,751 --> 01:18:14,669
Sorry. I see.

825
01:18:24,805 --> 01:18:26,849
I love all this.

826
01:18:26,974 --> 01:18:29,810
I think it's wonderful.
I love all this.

827
01:18:49,957 --> 01:18:51,541
You're still there.

828
01:18:57,005 --> 01:19:00,217
Everyone's here.
We're just about to go in.

829
01:19:00,258 --> 01:19:02,177
I'm sorry.

830
01:19:05,013 --> 01:19:06,515
We're struggling
with this redrafting.

831
01:19:06,598 --> 01:19:09,226
Everybody's here...
Donald, Trevor...

832
01:19:09,351 --> 01:19:11,771
We've got to get it done
tonight.

833
01:19:14,774 --> 01:19:16,150
I'll keep your food hot.

834
01:19:16,275 --> 01:19:17,360
Fine.

835
01:19:18,569 --> 01:19:21,406
I'll get back as quick as I can.

836
01:20:00,070 --> 01:20:01,614
I just heard from Edward.

837
01:20:03,491 --> 01:20:05,577
He says the word is they're
going to offer you Health.

838
01:20:06,995 --> 01:20:08,496
That means
you'd be in the cabinet.

839
01:20:11,249 --> 01:20:13,710
When did this happen?

840
01:20:13,835 --> 01:20:16,755
It was rumored
a couple days ago.

841
01:20:16,838 --> 01:20:18,465
What will you say?

842
01:20:21,009 --> 01:20:22,260
I don't know.

843
01:20:24,971 --> 01:20:26,848
I really don't know anything.

844
01:20:31,729 --> 01:20:32,813
Why?

845
01:20:36,901 --> 01:20:38,069
Because...

846
01:20:42,740 --> 01:20:44,325
Because what?

847
01:20:51,207 --> 01:20:52,333
Maybe it's what you always say.

848
01:20:52,416 --> 01:20:55,503
Maybe I just don't
want to get to the top.

849
01:20:57,214 --> 01:20:58,674
Is that all?

850
01:21:07,557 --> 01:21:09,309
I can't explain.

851
01:21:47,765 --> 01:21:49,018
It's all right. I'll get it.

852
01:21:59,320 --> 01:22:00,446
It's nobody.

853
01:22:22,636 --> 01:22:24,888
This has just come up for you.

854
01:22:28,475 --> 01:22:29,893
It's been passed by security.

855
01:22:29,976 --> 01:22:32,354
They've put it through
the machine. It's quite safe.

856
01:22:35,732 --> 01:22:37,651
Excuse me.

857
01:23:17,484 --> 01:23:19,236
I have to go out for a while.

858
01:23:21,154 --> 01:23:23,532
I'll be back as soon as I can.

859
01:24:59,840 --> 01:25:01,217
Beautiful wedding cakes.

860
01:25:01,300 --> 01:25:04,428
- Wedding cakes.
- You look at those.

861
01:25:04,595 --> 01:25:05,888
Now...

862
01:25:05,972 --> 01:25:07,890
You look at
the banqueting brochures...

863
01:25:07,974 --> 01:25:09,725
and I'll look at plum-duff.

864
01:25:09,809 --> 01:25:11,143
Cheers.

865
01:25:13,229 --> 01:25:16,273
Your mother should be
choosing the caterers.

866
01:25:16,440 --> 01:25:17,608
She's happy to leave it to us.

867
01:25:17,650 --> 01:25:19,735
And the church.

868
01:25:19,819 --> 01:25:21,071
She's had enough.

869
01:25:21,154 --> 01:25:23,073
She said she's tried them all.

870
01:25:23,156 --> 01:25:25,993
No one service is better
than any one else.

871
01:25:26,076 --> 01:25:27,995
I suppose she should know,
if anyone.

872
01:25:30,247 --> 01:25:32,291
But you know,
Stephen and I know St. Anselms.

873
01:25:32,457 --> 01:25:34,584
We do?

874
01:25:34,668 --> 01:25:36,086
The groom's father.
Good evening.

875
01:25:37,296 --> 01:25:39,464
We were just discussing
the wedding.

876
01:25:39,589 --> 01:25:41,216
We're not having
a white wedding...

877
01:25:41,300 --> 01:25:43,885
as I've already
moved in with Anna.

878
01:25:44,011 --> 01:25:47,307
Come on. You said
we'd go through the guest list.

879
01:25:47,348 --> 01:25:50,101
I will as long as I can
weed it out.

880
01:25:50,226 --> 01:25:51,311
Of course.

881
01:25:52,729 --> 01:25:54,647
It's actually quite fun.

882
01:26:14,168 --> 01:26:16,170
Do you think
I would have married Martyn...

883
01:26:17,546 --> 01:26:19,173
if I couldn't be with you?

884
01:26:23,594 --> 01:26:25,054
I'm coming.

885
01:26:48,286 --> 01:26:49,371
Hi, Jenny.

886
01:26:56,044 --> 01:26:58,546
We've done everything we can.

887
01:26:58,588 --> 01:26:59,923
As soon as possible.

888
01:27:00,006 --> 01:27:02,008
Couldn't be too soon for us.

889
01:27:02,092 --> 01:27:04,010
Everything
will be fine tomorrow.

890
01:27:04,094 --> 01:27:05,220
How's Rachel?

891
01:27:23,280 --> 01:27:25,157
It's Martyn.
Can I speak to Anna?

892
01:27:25,282 --> 01:27:27,118
I'm afraid she's left.

893
01:33:35,375 --> 01:33:37,168
Excuse me, please.

894
01:33:39,170 --> 01:33:40,296
Come on.

895
01:34:10,412 --> 01:34:12,497
Miss Barton
was to marry your son?

896
01:34:14,833 --> 01:34:16,918
You were here in the flat?

897
01:34:20,714 --> 01:34:23,133
Your son didn't know...

898
01:34:23,216 --> 01:34:25,761
He didn't know
of your relationship?

899
01:34:30,974 --> 01:34:32,100
You sure?

900
01:34:33,518 --> 01:34:34,603
You were careful?

901
01:34:37,565 --> 01:34:38,816
We were careful.

902
01:34:42,195 --> 01:34:43,946
Where did Miss Barton go?

903
01:34:47,325 --> 01:34:48,409
Sir?

904
01:34:51,120 --> 01:34:52,580
Do you know why she left?

905
01:35:18,273 --> 01:35:21,568
Do you have any comment
to make at this time?

906
01:35:21,652 --> 01:35:25,739
Have you got a statement
for us, please, sir?

907
01:35:25,823 --> 01:35:27,116
Come on.

908
01:35:35,792 --> 01:35:37,794
Stay right behind the line
of the gate, please.

909
01:35:42,173 --> 01:35:43,967
There are people living here.

910
01:35:49,556 --> 01:35:50,974
How are you feeling now?

911
01:35:52,017 --> 01:35:54,603
What does your wife think, sir?

912
01:36:11,829 --> 01:36:12,955
What have you done?

913
01:36:15,124 --> 01:36:16,834
The pain was unbearable.

914
01:36:18,502 --> 01:36:20,129
I was beating myself.

915
01:36:24,343 --> 01:36:26,094
The owner of the flat
called at the mews...

916
01:36:26,178 --> 01:36:29,848
to give Anna instructions
on how to work the boiler.

917
01:36:32,434 --> 01:36:34,978
It just happened
that Martyn was there.

918
01:36:38,690 --> 01:36:40,567
He'd never heard of the flat.

919
01:36:42,861 --> 01:36:44,321
Never heard of it.

920
01:36:46,907 --> 01:36:48,951
He took the address.

921
01:36:49,035 --> 01:36:50,662
It was chance.

922
01:36:54,957 --> 01:36:56,834
Why didn't you kill yourself?

923
01:36:59,128 --> 01:37:01,172
You should have killed yourself
when it began.

924
01:37:04,133 --> 01:37:05,468
Didn't you know?

925
01:37:11,140 --> 01:37:13,059
You thought you could go on?

926
01:37:17,022 --> 01:37:20,192
Every day into the future?

927
01:37:20,275 --> 01:37:22,861
Go on betraying us both
every day?

928
01:37:25,572 --> 01:37:27,574
You are not an evil man.

929
01:37:29,576 --> 01:37:32,413
You should have killed yourself
when you first realized...

930
01:37:32,496 --> 01:37:35,040
and then I would have
been able to mourn.

931
01:37:36,625 --> 01:37:40,422
It would have been hard,
but I would have buried you.

932
01:37:40,547 --> 01:37:42,424
And I would have wept.

933
01:38:05,905 --> 01:38:07,450
Take these.

934
01:38:07,575 --> 01:38:08,617
I don't...

935
01:38:08,743 --> 01:38:10,161
Come on.

936
01:38:10,286 --> 01:38:12,663
...want pills.
I want him back!

937
01:38:12,788 --> 01:38:14,665
I want my son back!

938
01:38:14,790 --> 01:38:18,336
I want Martyn!

939
01:38:18,461 --> 01:38:22,089
Give me my son!

940
01:38:25,134 --> 01:38:28,512
Martyn is dead.
Your son is dead.

941
01:38:28,637 --> 01:38:31,557
Give his death to me.
Give it to me.

942
01:38:35,979 --> 01:38:38,106
Give me his death.

943
01:38:41,985 --> 01:38:43,779
Give it to me.

944
01:39:18,356 --> 01:39:20,149
It's true, isn't it?

945
01:39:36,750 --> 01:39:38,669
What a pity we ever met.

946
01:40:22,048 --> 01:40:25,384
I think for everyone
there's just one person.

947
01:40:26,635 --> 01:40:28,095
For me...

948
01:40:29,472 --> 01:40:31,057
it was Martyn.

949
01:40:33,100 --> 01:40:35,227
For you, Anna.

950
01:40:36,645 --> 01:40:37,980
Poor Anna.

951
01:40:40,649 --> 01:40:41,734
Who?

952
01:40:46,948 --> 01:40:49,409
Were you ever in love with me...

953
01:40:52,913 --> 01:40:54,373
and this?

954
01:40:58,293 --> 01:40:59,753
Not enough?

955
01:40:59,878 --> 01:41:02,005
Not enough for you?

956
01:41:08,804 --> 01:41:11,431
For a moment there,
I thought of making love to you.

957
01:41:26,030 --> 01:41:29,117
Time. Grand healer, time.

958
01:41:29,200 --> 01:41:31,744
Time, not a day and a night.

959
01:41:49,179 --> 01:41:50,806
Come through, gentlemen.

960
01:42:10,160 --> 01:42:14,706
Gentlemen, in order to protect
my family and my colleagues...

961
01:42:14,873 --> 01:42:17,834
I am this morning
tendering my resignation.

962
01:42:35,311 --> 01:42:37,188
Come in.

963
01:42:46,906 --> 01:42:49,450
I tried to warn you.
I did try to warn you.

964
01:42:50,660 --> 01:42:52,078
I know.

965
01:43:01,254 --> 01:43:02,381
Do you know where she's gone?

966
01:43:06,093 --> 01:43:07,219
Where?

967
01:43:09,429 --> 01:43:10,597
She came here last night.

968
01:43:10,681 --> 01:43:12,641
She wanted to stay with me.

969
01:43:20,190 --> 01:43:21,274
Did she...

970
01:43:26,239 --> 01:43:27,782
I wonder,
did she leave a message?

971
01:43:43,506 --> 01:43:45,466
I do need to know
where she's gone.

972
01:43:45,591 --> 01:43:46,926
I think you know.

973
01:43:47,969 --> 01:43:50,221
You must remember.

974
01:43:50,304 --> 01:43:52,891
She told you the story
of what happened last time.

975
01:43:54,226 --> 01:43:55,769
She'll go back to Peter.

976
01:43:58,147 --> 01:44:00,149
You must know that.

977
01:44:08,032 --> 01:44:09,116
I must go.

978
01:44:10,284 --> 01:44:11,702
I am sorry.

979
01:44:11,785 --> 01:44:13,829
Good-bye.
We shan't meet again.

980
01:44:13,954 --> 01:44:15,205
Thank you.

981
01:44:23,173 --> 01:44:24,549
She's here, isn't she?

982
01:44:28,136 --> 01:44:29,220
She's here.

983
01:45:14,559 --> 01:45:18,230
It takes a remarkably short time
to withdraw from the world.

984
01:45:19,773 --> 01:45:21,858
I traveled...

985
01:45:21,942 --> 01:45:25,404
until I arrived
at a life of my own.

986
01:45:29,741 --> 01:45:33,078
What really makes us
is beyond grasping.

987
01:45:33,120 --> 01:45:36,708
It's way beyond knowing.

988
01:45:36,833 --> 01:45:38,042
We give in to love...

989
01:45:38,084 --> 01:45:41,713
because it gives us some sense
of what is unknowable.

990
01:45:43,381 --> 01:45:47,385
Nothing else matters,
not at the end.

991
01:47:37,624 --> 01:47:40,335
I saw her once more only.

992
01:47:40,460 --> 01:47:46,258
I saw her by accident
at an airport, changing planes.

993
01:47:51,556 --> 01:47:52,974
She didn't see me.

994
01:47:54,892 --> 01:47:56,394
She was with Peter.

995
01:47:57,520 --> 01:47:59,313
She was holding a child.

996
01:48:01,607 --> 01:48:04,068
She was no different
from anyone else.
